</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-3">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
If <m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> is a unit vector and <m>f</m>
has continuous second partials, then
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f(x,y) = f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2,
</md>
where <m>D^2_{\hat{u}} f = D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
We have that
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[a f_x + b f_y\right].
</md>
This simplifies to
<md>
\langle a f_{xx} + b f_{xy},\; a f_{xy} + b f_{yy}\rangle
\cdot \langle a, b\rangle
= f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2.
</md>
Note we needed <m>f</m> to have continuous second partials to invoke
Clairaut's Theorem.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
<m>D_{\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}}\,f(x,y) = f_x + f_y</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Since
<m>\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>,
<md>
D_{\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}}\,f(x,y)
= \nabla f(x,y) \cdot \langle 1, 1\rangle
= f_x + f_y.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-2">
<title>A mountain lion on a mountain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A mountain lion runs on a mountain whose height above the point
<m>(x,y)</m> is <m>z = x^2 + \sin^2(xy)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at the fastest
possible rate? What is this fastest rate?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at <em>half</em> of
the fastest possible rate?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The fastest possible rate of increase is attained in the direction of
the gradient:
<md>
\nabla z = \left\langle \frac{\partial z}{\partial x},
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right\rangle
= \left\langle 2x + 2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,y,\;
2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,x\right\rangle.
</md>
At <m>(1, 0, 1)</m>, this gradient is simply
<m>\nabla z(1,0) = \langle 2, 0\rangle</m>, and the fastest possible
rate of change is the length of the gradient, i.e.
<m>\|\nabla z(1,0)\| = \|\langle 2,0\rangle\| = 2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Half of the fastest possible rate of height increase at point
<m>(1,0,1)</m> is <m>\frac12 \cdot 2 = 1</m>. Let
<m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> be a unit vector in the desired
direction. Then the directional derivative
<m>D_{\hat{u}}(1,0) = 1</m> is the desired rate of change of height at
<m>(1,0,1)</m> in the direction of <m>\hat{u}</m>; it can be calculated
using the dot product with the gradient:
<md>
1 = D_{\hat{u}}(1,0)
= \hat{u} \cdot \nabla z(1,0)
= \langle a, b\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0\rangle
= 2a + 0b = 2a.
</md>
Thus, <m>a = \frac12</m> and <m>b</m> must be such that
<m>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac12, b\right\rangle</m> is unit, i.e.
<m>\left(\frac12\right)^2 + b^2 = 1</m>, from which
<m>b^2 = \frac34</m> and <m>b = \pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. Therefore,
the desired directions in which the height is increasing at a rate
equal to half of the fastest possible rate are
<m>\hat{u}_1 = \left\langle \frac12, \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>
and
<m>\hat{u}_2 = \left\langle \frac12, -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-6">
<statement>
<p>
A metal plate is placed on the <m>xy</m>-plane in such a way that the
temperature <m>T</m> at any point <m>(x,y)</m> is given by
<m>T = e^x(\sin x + \sin y)</m> <degree/>C.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
What is the rate of change in temperature at <m>(0,0)</m> in the
direction of <m>3\,\hat{\imath} - 4\,\hat{\jmath}</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(0,0)</m>, in what direction is the temperature increasing most
rapidly?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(0,0)</m>, in what direction is the temperature decreasing most
rapidly?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We first compute the gradient:
<md>
<mrow>T_x \amp = e^x(\sin x + \sin y) + e^x\cos x</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = e^x(\sin x + \cos x + \sin y),
\qquad T_x(0,0) = 1,</mrow>
<mrow>T_y \amp = e^x \cos y,
\qquad T_y(0,0) = 1,</mrow>
</md>
so <m>\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>. The direction
vector is <m>\overrightarrow{u} = \langle 3, -4\rangle</m> with
<m>\|\overrightarrow{u}\| = \sqrt{9 + 16} = 5</m>, so the unit vector is
<m>\hat u = \dfrac{\overrightarrow{u}}{\|\overrightarrow{u}\|}
= \left\langle \dfrac35, -\dfrac45\right\rangle</m>. Hence
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} T\right)_{(0,0)}
= \nabla T\big|_{(0,0)} \cdot \hat u
= \langle 1, 1\rangle \cdot
\left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle
= -\frac15.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature increases most rapidly in the direction of the
gradient:
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}}{\left\|\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}\right\|}
= \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to
the gradient:
<md>
\hat u = -\frac{\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}}{\left\|\nabla T\big|_{(0,0)}\right\|}
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-3">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y,z)</m> is a function of three variables and let
<md>
\hat u = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1, 0\rangle, \qquad
\hat v = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle, \qquad
\hat w = \langle 0, 0, 1\rangle.
</md>
Suppose that at a point <m>P_0(a,b,c)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat w} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 1.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find <em>the direction</em> of the maximum rate of change at
<m>P_0(a,b,c)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0(a,b,c)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Using
<m>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0}
= \nabla f\big|_{P_0}\cdot\hat u</m>, the three conditions become
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} = 0
\;\Rightarrow\;
\langle f_x, f_y, f_z\rangle \cdot
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1, 0\rangle
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(f_x - f_y\right) = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\qquad \Rightarrow\; f_x = f_y,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} = 0
\;\Rightarrow\;
\langle f_x, f_y, f_z\rangle \cdot
\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, 0, -1\rangle
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(f_x - f_z\right) = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\qquad \Rightarrow\; f_x = f_z,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat w} f\right)_{P_0} = 1
\;\Rightarrow\;
\langle f_x, f_y, f_z\rangle \cdot \langle 0, 0, 1\rangle
= f_z = 1.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>\nabla f\big|_{P_0} = \langle 1, 1, 1\rangle</m>, and the
direction of the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0</m> is given by
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla f\big|_{P_0}}{\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt3}\langle 1, 1, 1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\| = \sqrt{1 + 1 + 1} = \sqrt3</m>
is the maximum rate of change.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-7">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the maximum rate of change of <m>f(x,y) = \ln(x^2+y^2)</m>
at the point <m>(-4, 3)</m> and determine the direction in which it
occurs.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
\nabla f = \left(\frac{2x}{x^2+y^2}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{2y}{x^2+y^2}\right)\hat{\jmath},
</md>
so at <m>(-4,3)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f(-4,3)
\amp = \left(-\frac{8}{25}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(\frac{6}{25}\right)\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\|\nabla f(-4,3)\|
\amp = \sqrt{\frac{100}{(25)^2}} = \frac{10}{25}
= \frac25.</mrow>
</md>
The direction of maximum increase is
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla f(-4,3)}{\|\nabla f(-4,3)\|}
= \left(-\frac45\right)\hat{\imath} + \left(\frac35\right)\hat{\jmath},
</md>
and the maximum rate of increase is
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} = \|\nabla f(-4,3)\| = \frac25.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-8">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 e^{3y}</m> and the point
<m>P_0 = (-1, 0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the direction in which <m>f(x,y)</m> decreases the most rapidly
at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the
direction given by <m>\overrightarrow{v} = \langle -1, 1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = \left(2x e^{3y}\right)\hat{\imath}
+ \left(3x^2 e^{3y}\right)\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)} \amp = -2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath},
\qquad
\left\|\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)}\right\| = \sqrt{13}.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction of most rapid decrease is opposite to the gradient:
<md>
-\frac{\nabla f}{\|\nabla f\|}
= \left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\imath}
- \left(\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\jmath}
= \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}
\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The unit vector in the direction of <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is
<m>\hat u = \dfrac{\overrightarrow{v}}{\|\overrightarrow{v}\|}
= \left\langle -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</m>,
so
<md>
D_{\hat u} f
= \nabla f \cdot \hat u
= \langle -2, 3\rangle \cdot
\left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle
= \frac{5}{\sqrt2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-7">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the directions in which <m>f(x,y)</m> increases and decreases
most rapidly at <m>P_0(-1,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the derivatives of <m>f(x,y)</m> in these directions at
<m>P_0(-1,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = (2x + y)\,\hat{\imath} + (x + 2y)\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \amp = \langle -1, 1\rangle,
\qquad
\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\| = \sqrt2.</mrow>
</md>
The function increases most rapidly in the direction of the gradient,
<md>
\hat u_1 = \frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}}
{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|}
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle,
</md>
and decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction,
<md>
\hat u_2 = -\frac{\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}}
{\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|}
= \left\langle \frac{1}{\sqrt2}, -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The directional derivatives are
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u_1} f\right)_{P_0}
\amp = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \cdot \hat u_1
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2} + \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
= \sqrt2
= \left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u_2} f\right)_{P_0}
\amp = \left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)} \cdot \hat u_2
= -\frac{1}{\sqrt2} - \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
= -\sqrt2
= -\left\|\left(\nabla f\right)_{(-1,1)}\right\|.</mrow>
</md>
As expected, the largest possible rate of change is
<m>\|\nabla f\|</m> and the smallest is <m>-\|\nabla f\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-8">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) at a point
<m>(x,y,z)</m> is given by <m>T(x,y,z)</m>, where <m>T</m> is a
continuous function that has continuous partial derivatives, and
distances in <m>xyz</m>-space are measured in meters. We are given the
following information about <m>T</m> at the point
<m>(x,y,z) = (1,1,1)</m>:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>T(1,1,1) = 20</m> <degree/>C,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1,1)</m> obtains its smallest
(i.e. most negative) value in the direction toward the <em>point</em>
<m>(-1, 2, -3)</m>,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1,1)</m> in the direction of the
<em>vector</em> <m>-\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath} + 2\,\hat{k}</m> is
<m>2</m> <degree/>C/m.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1,1)</m> in the direction
toward the <em>point</em> <m>(-2, 1, 5)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If you start at <m>(1,1,1)</m> and move along a straight path toward
the point <m>(-2,1,5)</m>, <em>approximately</em> how far will you
have to go in this direction until the temperature reaches
<m>24</m> <degree/>C?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First we determine <m>\nabla T</m> at <m>(1,1,1)</m>. The derivative
is most negative in the direction of <m>-\nabla T</m>, and the
direction from <m>(1,1,1)</m> toward <m>(-1,2,-3)</m> is
<md>
\langle -1-1,\; 2-1,\; -3-1\rangle = \langle -2, 1, -4\rangle.
</md>
Hence <m>-\nabla T</m> is a positive multiple of
<m>\langle -2, 1, -4\rangle</m>, i.e.
<m>\nabla T = c\,\langle 2, -1, 4\rangle</m> for some <m>c \gt 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
The third piece of information fixes <m>c</m>. The unit vector in the
direction of <m>-\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath} + 2\,\hat{k}</m> is
<m>\frac13\langle -1, 2, 2\rangle</m>, since
<m>\|\langle -1,2,2\rangle\| = 3</m>, so
<md>
2 = \nabla T \cdot \frac13\langle -1, 2, 2\rangle
= \frac{c}{3}\Big(-2 - 2 + 8\Big)
= \frac{4c}{3}
\;\Rightarrow\; c = \frac32.
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\nabla T\big|_{(1,1,1)}
= \frac32\langle 2, -1, 4\rangle
= \left\langle 3, -\frac32, 6\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction from <m>(1,1,1)</m> toward <m>(-2,1,5)</m> is
<m>\langle -3, 0, 4\rangle</m>, whose length is <m>5</m>, so the unit
vector is <m>\hat u = \left\langle -\frac35, 0, \frac45\right\rangle</m>
and
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} T\right)_{(1,1,1)}
= \left\langle 3, -\frac32, 6\right\rangle \cdot
\left\langle -\frac35, 0, \frac45\right\rangle
= -\frac95 + \frac{24}{5}
= 3 \; {}^{\circ}\text{C}/\text{m}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature must rise from <m>20</m> <degree/>C to <m>24</m>
<degree/>C, i.e. <m>\Delta T = 4</m> <degree/>C, and along this
direction it rises at approximately <m>3</m> <degree/>C per meter.
Using the linear approximation
<m>\Delta T \approx \left(D_{\hat u}T\right) \cdot s</m>, where
<m>s</m> is the distance travelled,
<md>
s \approx \frac{\Delta T}{D_{\hat u}T} = \frac43 \approx 1.33
\text{ meters}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-30">
<statement>
<p>
In <m>xyz</m>-space, the temperature at the point <m>(x,y,z)</m> is
given by <m>T(x,y,z) = 4xy + 3z^2</m>. You're at <m>(2,0,1)</m> where
the temperature is <m>3^{\circ}</m>. Brrrr!
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In what direction should you go, from <m>(2,0,1)</m>, in order to warm
up the fastest?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
What is the directional derivative in that direction at the point
<m>(2,0,1)</m>?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If you walk <m>0.2</m> units in that direction, by about how many
degrees will you warm up?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
A function increases fastest in the direction of its gradient:
<md>
\nabla T = \langle 4y, 4x, 6z\rangle,
\qquad
\nabla T\big|_{(2,0,1)} = \langle 0, 8, 6\rangle
= 8\,\hat{\jmath} + 6\,\hat{k}.
</md>
So head in the direction <m>8\,\hat{\jmath} + 6\,\hat{k}</m>
(equivalently, the unit vector
<m>\left\langle 0, \frac45, \frac35\right\rangle</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In the direction of the gradient, the directional derivative equals
the length of the gradient:
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u}T\right)_{(2,0,1)}
= \left\|\nabla T\big|_{(2,0,1)}\right\|
= \sqrt{64 + 36} = 10.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The directional derivative is the rate of change of temperature per
unit of distance travelled, so by the linear approximation
<md>
\Delta T \approx \left(D_{\hat u}T\right) \cdot s
= 10 \times 0.2 = 2^{\circ}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-31">
<statement>
<p>
<m>f</m> is a <q>nice</q> function of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> (i.e.
<m>f</m> is continuous and its partials exist). In
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours"/> are shown the level curves
<m>f(x,y) = 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Let <m>u_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\imath} + \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\hat{\jmath}</m>.
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>D_{u_1}f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Draw on the diagram a unit vector pointing in the same direction as
<m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>P</m>, draw a unit vector and label it <m>u_2</m>, such that
<m>D_{u_2}f(P) \lt 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Estimate <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Which appears bigger, <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> or
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R)</m>?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours">
<caption>
Level curves of <m>f</m> at the values
<m>0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12</m>, with the three marked points <m>P</m>,
<m>Q</m>, and <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-2" function="c2" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-4" function="c4" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-6" function="c6" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-8" function="c8" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-10" function="c10" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ct-12" function="c12" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<point at="ct-P" p="(2.4,5.70)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-Q" p="(5.2,0.60)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<point at="ct-R" p="(4.6,4.35)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="ct-P-lab" anchor="(2.35,5.8)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-Q-lab" anchor="(5.2,-0.95)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
<m>Q</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-R-lab" anchor="(4.5,4.45)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>R</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l12" anchor="(6.45,4.06)" alignment="e">
<m>f=12</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l10" anchor="(6.45,3.14)" alignment="e">
<m>f=10</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l8" anchor="(6.45,2.04)" alignment="e">
<m>f=8</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l6" anchor="(6.45,0.91)" alignment="e">
<m>f=6</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l4" anchor="(6.45,0.50)" alignment="e">
<m>f=4</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l2" anchor="(6.45,-0.27)" alignment="e">
<m>f=2</m>
</label>
<label at="ct-l0" anchor="(6.45,-0.85)" alignment="e">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Seven level curves of f, labelled zero through twelve from the bottom up. Each curve rises steeply from the left, reaches a hump near x equals two point four, and then descends gently to the right. The low valued curves are bunched tightly together near the x axis on the right, where the point Q sits, while the high valued curves are widely spaced near the top right, where the point R sits. The point P sits at the very top of the f equals twelve curve, where that curve is horizontal.">
  <annotation ref="ct-P" text="The point P, at the peak of the f equals twelve level curve, where the level curve is horizontal."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-Q" text="The point Q, in the region at the lower right where the level curves are packed closely together."/>
  <annotation ref="ct-R" text="The point R, at the upper right where the level curves are far apart."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The directional derivative <m>D_{u_1}f(4,3)</m> tells us roughly how
much <m>f</m> increases if we move <em>one unit</em> from
<m>(4,3)</m> in the direction <m>u_1</m>, i.e. diagonally up and to
the right at <m>45^{\circ}</m>. Starting at <m>(4,3)</m> and moving
one unit that way lands us near <m>(4.7, 3.7)</m>, which is about two
contours further along, and each contour step is <m>\Delta f = 2</m>
spread over roughly one unit of travel. Reading the map,
<md>
D_{u_1}f(4,3) \approx 2.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve through that point is climbing at
roughly <m>45^{\circ}</m>, i.e. the level curve is
<em>parallel</em> to <m>u_1</m>. Moving along a level curve does not
change the value of <m>f</m>, so
<md>
D_{u_1}f(1,2) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The gradient is perpendicular to the level curve and points in the
direction of <em>increase</em>. At <m>(1,2)</m> the level curve runs
up and to the right at about <m>45^{\circ}</m>, and <m>f</m>
increases as we move upward, so <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points up and to
the <em>left</em>, i.e. in the direction
<m>\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle -1, 1\rangle</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We need any direction of <em>decrease</em> at <m>P</m>, i.e. any
direction pointing toward the lower-valued level curves (toward
<m>f = 10</m>, not away from it). Since the level curves below
<m>P</m> have smaller values, <m>u_2 = \langle 0, -1\rangle</m>
(straight down) works. See <xref ref="fig-pp-contours-answer"/>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>P</m> sits at the very top of its level curve, where the curve is
<em>horizontal</em>. Walking in the <m>+x</m> direction from
<m>P</m> therefore keeps us on the same level curve for a while, so
<m>f</m> does not change to first order:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(P) \approx 0.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> measures how fast <m>f</m>
changes as we move straight up, which is large where the contours are
close together and small where they are far apart. At <m>Q</m> we only
have to go up about <m>0.25</m> units to move from one contour to the
next, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q) \approx \dfrac{2}{0.25} = 8</m>.
At <m>R</m> we must go up about <m>1</m> unit for the same change, so
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(R) \approx \dfrac{2}{1} = 2</m>.
Hence <m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y}(Q)</m> is bigger.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pp-contours-answer">
<caption>
The vectors asked for in parts C and D: <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m> points
perpendicular to the level curve toward increasing <m>f</m>, while
<m>u_2</m> at <m>P</m> points toward decreasing <m>f</m>.
</caption>
<image width="88%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-pp-contours-answer">
<diagram dimensions="(470,420)" margins="12">
<definition>c0(t) = (t, -0.30 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c2(t) = (t, -0.08 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c4(t) = (t, 0.17 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c6(t) = (t, 0.75 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c8(t) = (t, 1.88 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c10(t) = (t, 2.98 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<definition>c12(t) = (t, 3.90 + 2.2/((1 + exp(-(t-1)/0.55))*(1 + exp((t-4.2)/0.9))))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.35,8.3,6.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-0" function="c0" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-2" function="c2" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-4" function="c4" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-6" function="c6" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-8" function="c8" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-10" function="c10" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="ca-12" function="c12" domain="(0.15,6.3)"
                  stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5"/>
<vector at="ca-grad" v="(-0.71,0.71)" tail="(1,2)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="ca-u2" v="(0,-1)" tail="(2.4,5.70)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="ca-pt12" p="(1,2)" fill="#009E73"/>
<point at="ca-P" p="(2.4,5.70)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="ca-grad-lab" anchor="(0.34,2.84)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
<m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>
</label>
<label at="ca-u2-lab" anchor="(2.5,4.75)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>u_2</m>
</label>
<label at="ca-P-lab" anchor="(2.3,5.8)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The same seven level curves, drawn in gray. A green arrow at the point one comma two points up and to the left, perpendicular to the level curve there; it is labelled gradient of f at one comma two. A vermillion arrow at P points straight down and is labelled u two.">
  <annotation ref="ca-grad" text="The gradient at one comma two, perpendicular to the level curve and pointing toward larger values of f."/>
  <annotation ref="ca-u2" text="The vector u two at P, pointing straight down toward smaller values of f, so the directional derivative there is negative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-32">
<statement>
<p>
A sheet of metal of varying density occupies the <m>xy</m>-plane. At
the point <m>(x,y)</m>, the density is
<m>100 + 0.1xy + 0.1y^2</m>. We move away from the point
<m>(10,10)</m> in the direction
<m>\frac35\hat{\imath} + \frac45\hat{\jmath}</m>. Use the directional
derivative to estimate about how far we'll have to go for the density
to increase by <m>6</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>\rho(x,y) = 100 + 0.1xy + 0.1y^2</m>. Then
<md>
\nabla \rho = \langle 0.1y,\; 0.1x + 0.2y\rangle,
\qquad
\nabla \rho\big|_{(10,10)} = \langle 1,\; 1 + 2\rangle
= \langle 1, 3\rangle.
</md>
The given direction is already a unit vector, since
<m>\left(\frac35\right)^2 + \left(\frac45\right)^2 = 1</m>, so
<md>
D_{\hat u}\rho(10,10)
= \langle 1, 3\rangle \cdot \left\langle \frac35, \frac45\right\rangle
= \frac35 + \frac{12}{5} = 3.
</md>
</p>

<p>
So the density increases at about <m>3</m> units of density per unit
of distance. To gain <m>6</m> units of density we therefore need to
travel approximately
<md>
s \approx \frac{6}{3} = 2 \text{ units}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<p>
The three problems that close this set repeat the three questions of
<xref ref="ex-two-runners"/> in a different physical setting: find the
direction of most rapid decrease, the rate along a prescribed direction,
and the directions of no change.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-electrostatic-plate">
  <title>Electric Potential on a Coated Plate</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The electric potential on a thin coated plate, in volts, is
      <md>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in centimeters. Consider the point <m>P_0(2,1)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find <m>\nabla V</m> at <m>P_0</m> and the electric field
            <m>\mathbf E = -\nabla V</m> there. Along which unit vector does the
            potential drop most rapidly, and at what rate?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A test charge is nudged along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{4}{5}\hat{\imath} - \tfrac{3}{5}\hat{\jmath}</m>. Compute
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}V</m> at <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the two unit vectors along which the potential does not change, and name
            the curve they are tangent to.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla V = -4x\,\hat{\imath} - 12y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla V)_{(2,1)} = -8\,\hat{\imath} - 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> and
            <m>\mathbf E = 8\,\hat{\imath} + 12\,\hat{\jmath}</m> V/cm. The potential drops
            fastest along <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath})</m>,
            at the rate <m>-\|\nabla V\| = -4\sqrt{13} \approx -14.4</m> V/cm.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <md>D_{\hat{u}}V = (-8)\left(\tfrac{4}{5}\right)
                + (-12)\left(-\tfrac{3}{5}\right) = \tfrac{4}{5} = 0.8</md>
            in V/cm. The potential rises slightly along this heading.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{13}}(3\,\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. These are
            tangent to the equipotential ellipse <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m> through <m>P_0</m>,
            and they are orthogonal to <m>\mathbf E</m>, as they must be.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-potential-plate">
      <caption>The potential <m>V(x,y) = 100 - 2x^2 - 6y^2</m> on the plate. One route holds <m>V = 86</m> V along the equipotential <m>2x^2 + 6y^2 = 14</m>; the other follows a field line, on which <m>D_{\hat{u}}V = -\|\nabla V\|</m> at every point.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-potential-plate"
                   iframe="potential-plate-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-fall-line">
  <title>The Fall Line on a Ski Slope</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A mountainside has elevation, in feet,
      <md>h(x,y) = 3000 - 0.0004x^2 - 0.001y^2,</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in feet. A skier stands at <m>P_0(200,100)</m>. Her
      gravitational potential energy is <m>U = mgh</m>, so the downhill force she feels is
      a positive multiple of <m>-\nabla h</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the <term>fall line</term>, the unit vector of steepest descent, and the
            grade of the slope there, expressed as a percent (feet dropped per hundred feet
            travelled).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A traverse is a route on which she neither climbs nor descends. Find the two
            unit vectors that accomplish this.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            She wants a gentler line of exactly <m>12\%</m> grade. Using
            <m>D_{\hat{u}}h = \|\nabla h\|\cos\theta</m>, find the angle <m>\theta</m>
            between her heading and the fall line.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\nabla h = -0.0008x\,\hat{\imath} - 0.002y\,\hat{\jmath}</m>, so
            <m>(\nabla h)_{(200,100)} = -0.16\,\hat{\imath} - 0.20\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. Steepest
            descent is along
            <m>\hat{u} = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{41}}(4\,\hat{\imath} + 5\,\hat{\jmath})</m>, and
            <m>\|\nabla h\| = \sqrt{0.0656} \approx 0.256</m>, a grade of about
            <m>25.6\%</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\hat{u} = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{41}}(5\,\hat{\imath} - 4\,\hat{\jmath})</m>, tangent
            to the contour line of constant elevation through <m>P_0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\cos\theta = 0.12/0.256 \approx 0.469</m>, so
            <m>\theta \approx 62^\circ</m> off the fall line, on either side.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
    <figure xml:id="figure-alpha-tangent">
      <caption>
        Part (a) says the grade is <m>\|\nabla h\| = 0.2561</m>. Here is what that
        number is, on the hill itself. The vertical plane through <m>P_0</m> in the
        direction <m>\hat{u}</m> cuts the surface in the gold curve; the red line
        is that curve's tangent at <m>P_0</m>; and <m>\alpha</m> is the angle it
        makes with the horizontal. Since <m>\tan\alpha = D_{\hat{u}}h</m>, the
        grade <em>is</em> the tangent of that angle. Swing <m>\hat{u}</m> with the
        slider: <m>\alpha</m> is largest along the fall line, where
        <m>\tan\alpha = 0.2561</m> and <m>\alpha = 14.4^\circ</m>, and it closes to
        nothing along the contour, where the tangent line lies flat.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-alpha-tangent"
                   iframe="alpha-tangent.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="1:1"/>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="figure-fall-line">
      <caption>The mountainside <m>h(x,y) = 3000 - 0.0004x^2 - 0.001y^2</m>. Compare the fall line, the level traverse, and the <m>12\%</m> line. Watch the grade readout drift away from <m>12\%</m> as the skier leaves <m>P_0</m>, since the angle in part (c) is computed at <m>P_0</m> only.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-fall-line"
                   iframe="fall-line-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="ex-isobars-low">
  <title>Sailing the Isobars of a Low</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Near the center of a low-pressure system the sea-level pressure, in millibars, is
      modeled by
      <md>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004\left(x^2 + y^2\right),</md>
      with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in miles from the center of the low. A ship sits at
      <m>P_0(20,10)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the pressure at the ship and <m>\nabla P</m> there. The
            pressure-gradient force per unit volume of air is a positive multiple of
            <m>-\nabla P</m>; in which unit direction does it push, and what is
            <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> in millibars per mile?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A second ship 40 miles from the center reports a stronger wind. Using this
            model, explain why <m>\|\nabla P\|</m> supports that report.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The captain decides to hold a constant barometer reading. Give the two
            possible unit headings at <m>P_0</m>, and describe the shape of the full route.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  
  </statement>
  <answer>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>P(20,10) = 988 + 0.004(500) = 990</m> mb, and
            <m>\nabla P = 0.008x\,\hat{\imath} + 0.008y\,\hat{\jmath}</m> gives
            <m>(\nabla P)_{(20,10)} = 0.16\,\hat{\imath} + 0.08\,\hat{\jmath}</m>. The force
            points along
            <m>-\mathbf u = -\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(2\,\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath})</m>, that is,
            inward toward the low, and
            <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.08\sqrt{5} \approx 0.18</m> mb/mi.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Here <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> grows linearly with distance from
            the center, so at 40 miles it is <m>0.32</m> mb/mi, nearly double the value at
            the first ship. (Real storms reverse this trend close to the eye; the model is
            only reasonable over a limited annulus.)
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\mathbf u = \pm\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{5}}(\hat{\imath} - 2\,\hat{\jmath})</m>. Since the
            level curves of <m>P</m> are the circles <m>x^2+y^2 = \text{constant}</m>,
            holding the barometer steady means circling the storm at a fixed radius of
            <m>\sqrt{500} \approx 22.4</m> miles.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the interactive figure to explore the problem further geometrically,
      switching between the two headings and watching how the barometer responds as
      the ship moves.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="figure-isobars-low">
      <caption>The pressure field <m>P(x,y) = 988 + 0.004(x^2+y^2)</m> around a low. One heading circles the eye at constant <m>990</m> mb; the other runs outward along <m>\nabla P</m>. The distance readout shows why <m>\|\nabla P\| = 0.008r</m> grows with radius.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-isobars-low"
                   iframe="isobars-low-3d.html"
                   width="100%" aspect="16:10"/>
    </figure>
  </answer>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
